<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG BUILDS ONLY. The release policy is src/main/res/xml/, which permits
  cleartext to loopback and nothing else; a build-type source set replaces it
  wholesale, so this file never reaches a release APK.

  Why it exists: from M5 the app talks to an OpenCode server the user runs
  themselves, typically on a laptop on the same LAN, reached over plain HTTP at
  an address like http://192.168.1.20:4096. The release policy would refuse that
  connection outright.

  Why it is this broad: Android's network security config matches on hostnames,
  not address ranges, so "permit cleartext to RFC1918 only" cannot be expressed
  here, and the host is not known until the user types it. The alternatives were
  to demand HTTPS from a self-hosted dev server, or to relax the release policy
  as well. Restricting the relaxation to the build type that exists for testing
  is the narrower of the available options.

  This is NOT a weakening of TLS verification. Certificate validation for https://
  URLs is untouched - no custom trust anchors, no debug-overrides, no hostname
  verifier. What changes is only whether the plaintext scheme is permitted at
  all, and only in a build that is not shipped.

  See ADR-0018 and .claude/rules/android.md N5.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
